From f289766085f6e360762290f097e9a2b069190711 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Amir <hello@noury.ch>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 16:58:28 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 02/32] feat(frecency): rank suggestions by directory-aware
 usage history
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Adds an opt-out (useFrecency, default true) ranking boost based on how often
and how recently a command was actually run in the current directory.

- utils/frecency.ts: append-only JSONL at ~/.inshellisense/frecency.jsonl,
  loaded once per session. The ranking path is pure in-memory map lookups, so
  completion never touches disk. Writes are fire-and-forget.
- suggestion.ts: the two inline priority sorts are replaced by a single shared
  rankSuggestions(), so subcommand-driven and arg-driven completion cannot drift
  apart in how they order the same suggestion.
- pty.ts: records on PromptStarted, which is when a command has actually been
  executed rather than merely typed, and where the shell cwd is known.

The boost is bounded to 25 against the engine's own scale (options 45, spec
default 50, generators 60) so history can lift a suggestion without flattening
spec-declared intent. Hits decay with a 14-day half-life, and same-directory
hits count 4x global ones — without that weighting the log curve saturates and
a command used often elsewhere ranks nearly as high here as in its own project.

With no history every boost is 0, so ordering is unchanged: all 93 existing
snapshots pass untouched.
